    "content": "Boca is a Spanish noun primarily meaning \"mouth,\" used for both humans and animals. It also translates to \"entrance,\" \"opening\" (like a tunnel or bay), or a harbor inlet. Common synonyms include boca (Portuguese), entrada (entrance), or orificio (opening), while common phrases include boca a boca (word of mouth). Common Usage Examples: Anatomical: Abre la boca (Open your mouth). Inlet/Geographical: Boca Raton translates to \"Mouse Mouth\" or rocky inlet. Directional/Idiomatic: Boca arriba (face up), boca abajo (face down). Example Sentence: Es de mala educación hablar con la boca llena (It is impolite to speak with your mouth full). Key Details: Origin: Derived from the Latin word bucca (\"cheek\" or \"mouth\"). Gender: Feminine noun (la boca). Context: Used frequently in geography to define a river mouth or harbor entrance. Other contexts: In some contexts, it can also refer to a \"muzzle\" or \"trap\".",
